      "id": "p1-dynamic-workflow",
      "priority": "P1",
      "required": true,
      "evidenceStatus": "external-evidence-required",
      "evidenceNotes": "Repository tests close generated-draft review authority, exact admission/replay, serial and bounded-parallel request-before-provider persistence, atomic dispatch-batch identity, ordered multi-pending reconciliation, the pause settlement barrier, attempt-scoped retry/timeout recovery, effect-bound provider tracing for ordinary model turns plus semantic compaction, and runtime-derived child identities for directly dispatched tools. The dynamic runtime synchronously persists bounded provider/tool call rows before transport and their terminal settlement independently of the completed outer task result. A provider-returned trace receipt is validated against the exact started call and synchronously prewritten before the matching usage or unknown settlement, so a crash after provider response can leave a started row with bounded receipt identifiers. Terminal settlement rejects a returned receipt unless an identical prewrite already exists. Runtime observability now derives provider attempt and receipt lineage from those durable call rows, including pending/crashed calls, and ignores conflicting outer-result receipt arrays whenever durable rows exist. Pre-durable-call states remain readable only through an explicitly counted legacy result fallback that keeps a machine-readable gap. This includes descendant calls whose original request source differs from their subagent attribution; operator reconciliation preserves the prewritten receipt. Spawned sub-agents and isolated skills inherit the already-persisted parent tool child-effect as their own workflow owner, so descendant provider/tool calls enter the same store with a verified owner chain; orphan owners, receipt or owner tamper, swallowed unknown outcomes, and outer provider-return settlement over descendant unknown are rejected. MCP calls additionally persist the outer/child identity tuple in their session ledger before transport and expose persisted start/settlement flags. OpenAI request identifiers and receipts remain trace-only and are not independently readable. Release readiness still requires hook/checkpoint/artifact and other observer-bypassing side effects to consume governed authority, native third-party idempotency and independently readable receipts, complete artifact/checkpoint authority, dual-IDE consumption, plugin distribution, and the declared real-host matrix on the exact release commit.",
